Die Yield Prediction 
 
Problem Statement
Multi-Resolution Die Yield Prediction with Interpretable Spatial Context
 
Background
In semiconductor manufacturing, each wafer contains thousands of dies arranged in a grid. Each die undergoes electrical testing that produces multiple parametric measurements. Additionally, each die contains internal blocks that produce sub-die-level test readings — a high-dimensional signal that captures fine-grained process variation within the die. However, dies don't fail in isolation — failures are often influenced by neighbourhood effects (nearby defective dies), wafer-level process gradients (radial/linear variations from the chamber), and patterns hidden within block-level test readings.
 
Objective
Build two models and compare their performance:

Model A — Die-Level Only Predict per-die pass/fail probability using:
•	Die-level features (n parametric test measurements per die)
•	Spatial context (neighborhood pattern in the surrounding m×m window, derived from die positions)

Model B — Die + Block-Level Predict per-die pass/fail probability using everything in Model A, plus:
•	Block-level readings (a dense array of k test readings per die, capturing sub-die process variation)

The goal is to demonstrate whether and how much the high-dimensional block-level signal improves prediction over die-level features alone.

Sample Wafer Map Visualization
Below is an example wafer showing pre-test state, post-test state, and newly failed dies:
•	Green: Passing dies
•	Red: Pre-test failures (old_label = 1)
•	Orange: Newly failed dies after test (prediction target)

The challenge is to predict which green dies in the pre-test map will turn orange/red in the post-test map.

 
 
 
Key Challenges
1.	Extreme class imbalance — only ~2-5% of dies fail
2.	High overlap — most failing dies have feature distributions that heavily overlap with passing dies (marginal failures)
3.	High-dimensional block data — each die has ~2000 block readings; the model must handle dimensionality reduction, embedding, or direct modeling of this signal
4.	Interpretability required — for each predicted failure, the model must output which features (and which block-level patterns, for Model B) and which spatial context contributed most to the prediction (engineers need actionable root cause, not a black box)
 
Evaluation Criteria
Criterion	Weight
Prediction performance — Model A vs Model B comparison (F1, AUC-PR on minority class)	30%
Handling of imbalance (no trivial "predict all pass")	20%
Model interpretability (feature attribution, block pattern analysis, spatial heatmaps)		30%
Creative use of multi-resolution data fusion and improvement analysis (Model A → B delta)	20%


Data Approach
The WM-811K wafer dataset is used as the spatial foundation, with synthetic die-level parametric features and block-level readings to be generated on top of it.

Expected Deliverables
1.	Model A: A trained model that takes (m × n) die-feature matrix + spatial context → outputs (m × 1) failure probabilities
2.	Model B: A trained model that takes die-feature matrix + spatial context + block-level readings → outputs (m × 1) failure probabilities
3.	Comparison analysis: Side-by-side performance metrics (F1, AUC-PR, accuracy) showing improvement (or lack thereof) from adding block-level data
4.	Interpretability output: per-die feature importance + spatial contribution visualization (Model A), plus block reading pattern analysis (Model B)
5.	Analysis of how each model handles the imbalanced, overlapping distributions

Setup
1.	Download the provided zip file and extract it to a local directory
2.	Navigate to the extracted folder
3.	Create and activate a virtual environment (recommended):
•	Windows: python -m venv venv && venv\Scripts\activate
•	Linux/Mac: python3 -m venv venv && source venv/bin/activate
4.	Install dependencies: pip install -r requirements.txt
5.	Download the reference dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/qingyi/wm811k-wafer-map 
6.	Place LSWMD.pkl in data/ directory 
7.	Run generate_data.py to produce the training, test, and validation datasets

